            <p>
              
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="leastsquares-linear-eg">
      <title>Best-fit linear function</title>
      <idx><h>Best-fit problem</h><h>best-fit linear equation</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the linear function <m>f(x,y)</m> that best approximates the following data:
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{array}{r|r|c}
  x \amp y \amp f(x,y) \\\hline
  1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
  0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
  -1 \amp 0 \amp 3 \\
  0 \amp -1 \amp 4
\end{array}</mrow>
</md>
          What quantity is being minimized?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p component="default">
          The general equation for a linear function in two variables is
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = Bx + Cy + D</mrow>
</md>.
          We want to solve the following system of equations in the unknowns <m>B,C,D</m>:
          <md xml:id="leastsquares-linear-eq" number="yes">
<mrow>\spalignsysdelims..\left\{\begin{alignedat}{5}
B(1) \amp {}+{} \amp C(0) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
B(0) \amp {}+{} \amp C(1) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 1 \amp {} \\
B( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}+{} \amp C(0) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {}={} \amp 3 \amp {} \\
B(0) \amp {}+{} \amp C( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {}={} \amp 4 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right</mrow>
</md>.
          In matrix form, we can write this as <m>Ax=b</m> for
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad x = \begin{pmatrix}
B \\
C \\
D
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad b = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.

          We observe that the columns <m>u_1,u_2,u_3</m> of <m>A</m> are <em>orthogonal</em>, so we can use <xref ref="leastsquares-recipe2">recipe 2</xref>:
          <md>
<mrow>\hat x = \left(\frac{b\cdot u_1}{u_1\cdot u_1},\;
              \frac{b\cdot u_2}{u_2\cdot u_2},\;
              \frac{b\cdot u_3}{u_3\cdot u_3}
              \right)
            = \left(\frac{-3}{2},\;\frac{-3}{2},\;\frac{8}{4}\right)
            = \left(-\frac32,\;-\frac32,\;2\right)</mrow>
</md>.


          Therefore, the best-fit linear equation is
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = -\frac 32x - \frac32y + 2</mrow>
</md>.
          Here is a picture of the graph of <m>f(x,y)</m>:
          <latex-code mode="bare">
            \usetikzlibrary{math}
          </latex-code>
          </p>
<p component="1553">
          The general equation for a linear function in two variables is
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = Bx + Cy + D</mrow>
</md>.
          We want to solve the following system of equations in the unknowns <m>B,C,D</m>:
          <md xml:id="leastsquares-linear-eq" number="yes">
<mrow>\spalignsysdelims..\left\{\begin{alignedat}{5}
B(1) \amp {}+{} \amp C(0) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 0 \amp {} \\
B(0) \amp {}+{} \amp C(1) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp 1 \amp {} \\
B( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}+{} \amp C(0) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {}={} \amp 3 \amp {} \\
B(0) \amp {}+{} \amp C( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}+{} \amp D \amp {}={} \amp 4 \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right</mrow>
</md>.
          In matrix form, we can write this as <m>Ax=b</m> for
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
-1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
0 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad x = \begin{pmatrix}
B \\
C \\
D
\end{pmatrix}
            \qquad b = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1 \\
3 \\
4
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.


          We find a least-squares solution by multiplying both sides by the transpose:
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 4
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            A^Tb = \begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
-3 \\
8
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          The matrix <m>A^TA</m> is diagonal (do you see why that happened?), so it is easy to solve the equation <m>A^TAx = A^Tb</m>:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}
2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 8
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3/2 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -3/2 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2
\end{array}\right)
            \implies
            \hat x = \begin{pmatrix}
-3/2 \\
-3/2 \\
2
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.

          Therefore, the best-fit linear equation is
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = -\frac 32x - \frac32y + 2</mrow>
</md>.
          Here is a picture of the graph of <m>f(x,y)</m>:
          <latex-code mode="bare">
            \usetikzlibrary{math}
          </latex-code>
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1, myxyz, thin border nodes,
    vert lines/.style={very thin, black!40}]

  \begin{scope}[transformxy]
    \draw[help lines, black!90] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
  \end{scope}

  \tikzmath {
    int \x, \y;
    function f(\x,\y) {
      return -3/2*\x-3/2*\y+2;
    };
    for \x in {-2,...,1}{
      for \y in {-2,...,1}{
        { \filldraw[help lines, seq-red!70, fill=seq-red!40, fill opacity=.5]
             (\x,\y,{f(\x,\y)})
             -- (\x+1,\y,{f(\x+1,\y)})
             -- (\x+1,\y+1,{f(\x+1,\y+1)})
             -- (\x,\y+1,{f(\x,\y+1)})
             -- cycle;
        };
      };
    };
  }

  \draw[vert lines] (-2,-2,{f(-2,-2)}) -- (-2,-2,0);
  \draw[vert lines] (-2, 2,{f(-2, 2)}) -- (-2, 2,0);
  \draw[vert lines] ( 2,-2,{f( 2,-2)}) -- ( 2,-2,0);
  \draw[vert lines] ( 2, 2,{f( 2, 2)}) -- ( 2, 2,0);

  \begin{scope}[every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=1pt}]
    \draw[thick,seq-blue] (1,0,0)
      -- (1,0,{f(1,0)})
      node[point,seq-red,"${f(1,0)}$" text=seq-red] {};
    \point["${(1,0,0)}$" below] at (1,0,0);
    \draw[densely dotted] (0,1,1) -- (0,1,0);
    \draw[thick,seq-blue] (0,1,1)
      -- (0,1,{f(0,1)})
      node[point,seq-red,"${f(0,1)}$" {text=seq-red, below}] {};
    \point["${(0,1,1)}$"] at (0,1,1);
    \draw[densely dotted] (-1,0,3) -- (-1,0,0);
    \draw[thick,seq-blue] (-1,0,3)
      -- (-1,0,{f(-1,0)})
      node[point,seq-red,"${f(-1,0)}$" text=seq-red] {};
    \point["${(-1,0,3)}$" below] at (-1,0,3);
    \draw[densely dotted] (0,-1,4) -- (0,-1,0);
    \draw[thick,seq-blue] (0,-1,4)
      -- (0,-1,{f(0,-1)})
      node[point,seq-red,"${f(0,-1)}$" {text=seq-red, below}] {};
    \point["${(0,-1,4)}$"] at (0,-1,4);
  \end{scope}

  \point[scale=.75, black!50] at (0,0,0);

\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
        <p>
          Now we consider what quantity is being minimized by the function <m>f(x,y)</m>.  The least-squares solution <m>\hat x</m> minimizes the sum of the squares of the entries of the vector <m>b-A\hat x</m>.  The vector <m>b</m> is the right-hand side of <xref ref="leastsquares-linear-eq"/>, and
          <md>
<mrow>A\hat x =
            \spalignsysdelims()\left\{\begin{alignedat}{5}
-\frac32(1) \amp {}-{} \amp \frac32(0) \amp {}+{} \amp 2 \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp  \amp {} \\
-\frac32(0) \amp {}-{} \amp \frac32(1) \amp {}+{} \amp 2 \amp {} \amp {} \amp {}={} \amp  \amp {} \\
-\frac32( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}-{} \amp \frac32(0) \amp {}+{} \amp 2 \amp {}={} \amp  \amp {} \\
-\frac32(0) \amp {}-{} \amp \frac32( \amp {}-{} \amp 1) \amp {}+{} \amp 2 \amp {}={} \amp  \amp {}
\end{alignedat}\right.
            = \begin{pmatrix}
{f(1,0)} \\
{f(0,1)} \\
{f(-1,0)} \\
{f(0,-1)}
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          In other words, <m>A\hat x</m> is the vector whose entries are the values of <m>f</m> evaluated on the points <m>(x,y)</m> we specified in our data table, and <m>b</m> is the vector whose entries are the desired values of <m>f</m> evaluated at those points.  The difference <m>b-A\hat x</m> is the vertical distance of the graph from the data points, as indicated in the above picture.  The best-fit linear function minimizes the sum of these vertical distances.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The best-fit linear function minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances (violet).  Click and drag the points to see how the best-fit linear function changes.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/bestfit-if.html?func=A*x+B*y+C&amp;v1=1,0,0&amp;v2=0,1,1&amp;v3=-1,0,3&amp;v4=0,-1,4&amp;range=5"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      <idx><h>Best-fit problem</h><h>general setup</h></idx>
      All of the above examples have the following form: some number of data points <m>(x,y)</m> are specified, and we want to find a function
      <md>
<mrow>y = B_1g_1(x) + B_2g_2(x) + \cdots + B_mg_m(x)</mrow>
</md>
      that best approximates these points, where <m>g_1,g_2,\ldots,g_m</m> are fixed functions of <m>x</m>.  Indeed, in the best-fit line example we had <m>g_1(x)=x</m> and <m>g_2(x)=1</m>; in the best-fit parabola example we had <m>g_1(x)=x^2</m>, <m>g_2(x)=x</m>, and <m>g_3(x)=1</m>; and in the best-fit linear function example we had <m>g_1(x_1,x_2)=x_1</m>, <m>g_2(x_1,x_2)=x_2</m>, and <m>g_3(x_1,x_2)=1</m> (in this example we take <m>x</m> to be a vector with two entries).  We evaluate the above equation on the given data points to obtain a system of linear equations in the unknowns <m>B_1,B_2,\ldots,B_m</m><mdash/>once we evaluate the <m>g_i</m>, they just become numbers, so it does not matter what they are<mdash/>and we find the least-squares solution.  The resulting best-fit function minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances from the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> to our original data points.
    </p>

    <p>
      To emphasize that the nature of the functions <m>g_i</m> really is irrelevant, consider the following example.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Best-fit trigonometric function</title>
      <idx><h>Best-fit problem</h><h>best-fit trigonometric function</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the best-fit function of the form
          <md>
<mrow>y=B+C\cos(x)+D\sin(x)+E\cos(2x)+F\sin(2x)+G\cos(3x)+H\sin(3x)</mrow>
</md>
          passing through the points
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{pmatrix}
-4 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
-3 \\
0
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
-2 \\
-1.5
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
.5
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
1
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
2 \\
-.5
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
3 \\
2
\end{pmatrix},\;
            \begin{pmatrix}
4 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}?</mrow>
</md>
          <latex-code>
\def\labeledpt(#1)#2{\point["{$(#1)$}" #2] at (#1)}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.35, thin border nodes, whitebg nodes]
  \draw[grid lines] (-5,-2) grid (5,2);
  \draw[->] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-2) -- (0,2);

  \labeledpt(-4,-1){{below}};
  \labeledpt(-3,0){{below}};
  \labeledpt(-2,-1.5){below};
  \labeledpt(-1,.5){above};
  \labeledpt(0,1){{above}};
  \labeledpt(1,-1){below};
  \labeledpt(2,-.5){below};
  \labeledpt(3,2){below};
  \labeledpt(4,-1){above};

\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-code>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We want to solve the system of equations
          <latex-code>
            \small\[
            \spalignsysdelims..
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(-4) + D\sin(-4) + E\cos(-8) + F\sin(-8) + G\cos(-12) + H\sin(-12) \amp {} \\
0 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(-3) + D\sin(-3) + E\cos(-6) + F\sin(-6) + G\cos(-9) + H\sin(-9) \amp {} \\
-1.5 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(-2) + D\sin(-2) + E\cos(-4) + F\sin(-4) + G\cos(-6) + H\sin(-6) \amp {} \\
0.5 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(-1) + D\sin(-1) + E\cos(-2) + F\sin(-2) + G\cos(-3) + H\sin(-3) \amp {} \\
1 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(0) + D\sin(0) + E\cos(0) + F\sin(0) + G\cos(0) + H\sin(0) \amp {} \\
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(1) + D\sin(1) + E\cos(2) + F\sin(2) + G\cos(3) + H\sin(3) \amp {} \\
-0.5 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(2) + D\sin(2) + E\cos(4) + F\sin(4) + G\cos(6) + H\sin(6) \amp {} \\
2 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(3) + D\sin(3) + E\cos(6) + F\sin(6) + G\cos(9) + H\sin(9) \amp {} \\
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B + C\cos(4) + D\sin(4) + E\cos(8) + F\sin(8) + G\cos(12) + H\sin(12) \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right.\]
          </latex-code>
          All of the terms in these equations are <em>numbers</em>, except for the unknowns <m>B,C,D,E,F,G,H</m>:
          <latex-code>
            \small\[
            \spalignsysdelims..
            \left\{\begin{alignedat}{2}
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.6536C + 0.7568D - 0.1455E - 0.9894F + 0.8439G + 0.5366H \amp {} \\
0 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.9900C - 0.1411D + 0.9602E + 0.2794F - 0.9111G - 0.4121H \amp {} \\
-1.5 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.4161C - 0.9093D - 0.6536E + 0.7568F + 0.9602G + 0.2794H \amp {} \\
0.5 \amp {}={} \amp B + 0.5403C - 0.8415D - 0.4161E - 0.9093F - 0.9900G - 0.1411H \amp {} \\
1 \amp {}={} \amp B + C \. + E \. + G \amp {} \\
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B + 0.5403C + 0.8415D - 0.4161E + 0.9093F - 0.9900G + 0.1411H \amp {} \\
-0.5 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.4161C + 0.9093D - 0.6536E - 0.7568F + 0.9602G - 0.2794H \amp {} \\
2 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.9900C + 0.1411D + 0.9602E - 0.2794F - 0.9111G + 0.4121H \amp {} \\
-1 \amp {}={} \amp B - 0.6536C - 0.7568D - 0.1455E + 0.9894F + 0.8439G - 0.5366H \amp .
\end{alignedat}\right.\]
          </latex-code>
          Hence we want to solve the least-squares problem
          <latex-code>
            \small\[
            \begin{pmatrix}
1 \amp -0.6536 \amp 0.7568 \amp -0.1455 \amp -0.9894 \amp 0.8439 \amp 0.5366 \\
1 \amp -0.9900 \amp -0.1411 \amp 0.9602 \amp 0.2794 \amp -0.9111 \amp -0.4121 \\
1 \amp -0.4161 \amp -0.9093 \amp -0.6536 \amp 0.7568 \amp 0.9602 \amp 0.2794 \\
1 \amp 0.5403 \amp -0.8415 \amp -0.4161 \amp -0.9093 \amp -0.9900 \amp -0.1411 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
1 \amp 0.5403 \amp 0.8415 \amp -0.4161 \amp 0.9093 \amp -0.9900 \amp 0.1411 \\
1 \amp -0.4161 \amp 0.9093 \amp -0.6536 \amp -0.7568 \amp 0.9602 \amp -0.2794 \\
1 \amp -0.9900 \amp 0.1411 \amp 0.9602 \amp -0.2794 \amp -0.9111 \amp 0.4121 \\
1 \amp -0.6536 \amp -0.7568 \amp -0.1455 \amp 0.9894 \amp 0.8439 \amp -0.5366
\end{pmatrix}
            \begin{pmatrix}
B \\
C \\
D \\
E \\
F \\
G \\
H
\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}
-1 \\
0 \\
-1.5 \\
0.5 \\
1 \\
-1 \\
-0.5 \\
2 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}.
            \]
          </latex-code>
          We find the least-squares solution with the aid of a computer:
          <latex-code>
            \small\[
            \hat x \approx \begin{pmatrix}
-0.1435 \\
0.2611 \\
-0.2337 \\
1.116 \\
-0.5997 \\
-0.2767 \\
0.1076
\end{pmatrix}.
            \]
          </latex-code>
          Therefore, the best-fit function is
          <md>
<mrow>y \amp\approx
            -0.1435 + 0.2611\cos(x) -0.2337\sin(x)  + 1.116\cos(2x) -0.5997\sin(2x)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\qquad\qquad -0.2767\cos(3x) +  0.1076\sin(3x)</mrow>
</md>.
          <latex-code>
\def\labeledpt(#1)#2{\point["{$(#1)$}" #2] at (#1)}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.35, thin border nodes, whitebg nodes]
  \draw[grid lines] (-5,-2) grid (5,2);
  \draw[->] (-5,0) -- (5,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-2) -- (0,2);

  \labeledpt(-4,-1){{below,xshift=3mm}};
  \labeledpt(-3,0){{below,xshift=-2mm}};
  \labeledpt(-2,-1.5){below};
  \labeledpt(-1,.5){above left};
  \labeledpt(0,1){{above,xshift=3mm}};
  \labeledpt(1,-1){below left};
  \labeledpt(2,-.5){left};
  \labeledpt(3,2){below};
  \labeledpt(4,-1){above right};

  \draw[thick, seq-red, domain=-5:5, samples=100, smooth]
    plot (\x, {-0.14349970812998397 + 0.2610707891632625*cos(\x r)
                - 0.23369384976594704*sin(\x r) + 1.1164954570890104*cos(2*\x r)
                - 0.5996551982189049*sin(2*\x r) - 0.2767472417583463*cos(3*\x r)
                + 0.10760782737030863*sin(3*\x r)});
  \node[text=seq-red, font=\tiny, below] at (0, -2.3)
    {$y\approx -0.14 + 0.26\cos(x) - 0.23\sin(x) + 1.11\cos(2x)
      - 0.60\sin(2x) - 0.28\cos(3x) + 0.11\sin(3x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-code>
          As in the previous examples, the best-fit function minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances from the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> to the data points.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The best-fit function minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances (violet).  Click and drag the points to see how the best-fit function changes.</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/bestfit-if.html?func=A+B*cos(x)+C*sin(x)+D*cos(2*x)+EE*sin(2*x)+F*cos(3*x)+G*sin(3*x)&amp;v1=-4,-1&amp;v2=-3,0&amp;v3=-2,-1.5&amp;v4=-1,.5&amp;v5=0,1&amp;v6=1,-1&amp;v7=2,-.5&amp;v8=3,2&amp;v9=4,-1&amp;range=5"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The next example has a somewhat different flavor from the previous ones.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Best-fit ellipse</title>
      <idx><h>Best-fit problem</h><h>best-fit ellipse</h></idx>
      <idx><h>Ellipse</h><h>best-fit problem</h></idx>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the best-fit ellipse through the points
          <md>
<mrow>(0,2),\, (2,1),\, (1,-1),\, (-1,-2),\, (-3,1),\, (-1,-1)</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1,decoration={brace,raise=1mm},
    thin border nodes, whitebg nodes]
  \draw[grid lines] (-6,-4) grid (6,4);
  \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (6,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,4);

  \point["{$(0,2)$}" above] at (0,2);
  \point["{$(2,1)$}" right] at (2,1);
  \point["{$(1,-1)$}" {right}] at (1,-1);
  \point["{$(-1,-2)$}" {below,xshift=-1mm}] at (-1,-2);
  \point["{$(-3,1)$}" {above}] at (-3,1);
  \point["{$(-1,1)$}" below] at (-1,1);

\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
<p>
          What quantity is being minimized?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The general equation for an ellipse (actually, for a nondegenerate conic section) is
          <md>
<mrow>x^2 + By^2 + Cxy + Dx + Ey + F = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          This is an <em>implicit equation</em>: the ellipse is the set of all solutions of the equation, just like the unit circle is the set of solutions of <m>x^2+y^2=1.</m>  To say that our data points lie on the ellipse means that the above equation is satisfied for the given values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>:
          <md xml:id="leastsquares-ellipse-eq" number="yes">
<mrow>\def\eqline#1#2{(#1)^2 + B(#2)^2 + C(#1)(#2) + D(#1) + E(#2) + F = 0}
\edef\eqs{\eqline02;
  \eqline21;
  \eqline1{-1};
  \eqline{-1}{-2};
  \eqline{-3}1;
  \eqline{-1}{-1}
}
\spalignsysdelims..
\expandafter\syseq\expandafter{\eqs\rlap.}</mrow>
</md>
          To put this in matrix form, we move the constant terms to the right-hand side of the equals sign; then we can write this as <m>Ax=b</m> for
          <md>
<mrow>A = \begin{pmatrix}
4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp \phantom-1 \\
1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
4 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp -3 \amp -3 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            x = \begin{pmatrix}
B \\
C \\
D \\
E \\
F
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            b = \begin{pmatrix}
0 \\
-4 \\
-1 \\
-1 \\
-9 \\
-1
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We compute
          <md>
<mrow>A^TA = \begin{pmatrix}
36 \amp 7 \amp -5 \amp 0 \amp 12 \\
7 \amp 19 \amp 9 \amp -5 \amp 1 \\
-5 \amp 9 \amp 16 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\
0 \amp -5 \amp 1 \amp 12 \amp 0 \\
12 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 6
\end{pmatrix} \qquad
            A^T b = \begin{pmatrix}
-19 \\
17 \\
20 \\
-9 \\
-16
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>.
          We form an augmented matrix and row reduce:
          <md>
<mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrrrr|r}
36 \amp 7 \amp -5 \amp 0 \amp 12 \amp -19 \\
7 \amp 19 \amp 9 \amp -5 \amp 1 \amp 17 \\
-5 \amp 9 \amp 16 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 20 \\
0 \amp -5 \amp 1 \amp 12 \amp 0 \amp -9 \\
12 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 6 \amp -16
\end{array}\right) \rref
            \left(\begin{array}{rrrrr|r}
1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 405/266 \\
0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -89/133 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 201/133 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -123/266 \\
0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -687/133
\end{array}\right)</mrow>
</md>.
          The least-squares solution is
          <md>
<mrow>\hat x = \begin{pmatrix}
405/266 \\
-89/133 \\
201/133 \\
-123/266 \\
-687/133
\end{pmatrix}</mrow>
</md>,
          so the best-fit ellipse is
          <md>
<mrow>x^2 + \frac{405}{266} y^2 -\frac{89}{133} xy + \frac{201}{133}x
            - \frac{123}{266}y - \frac{687}{133} = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          Multiplying through by <m>266</m>, we can write this as
          <md>
<mrow>266 x^2 + 405 y^2 - 178 xy + 402 x - 123 y - 1374 = 0</mrow>
</md>.
          </p>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
<!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this TikZ figure (linear algebra context) -->
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
<!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this TikZ figure for a linear algebra course -->
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[thin border nodes, whitebg nodes]
  \draw[grid lines] (-6,-4) grid (6,4);
  \draw[->] (-6,0) -- (6,0);
  \draw[->] (0,-4) -- (0,4);

  \point["{$(0,2)$}" above] at (0,2);
  \point["{$(2,1)$}" right] at (2,1);
  \point["{$(1,-1)$}" {right,yshift=-1mm}] at (1,-1);
  \point["{$(-1,-2)$}" {below,xshift=1.5mm}] at (-1,-2);
  \point["{$(-3,1)$}" {above,xshift=-1.5mm}] at (-3,1);
  \point["{$(-1,1)$}" below] at (-1,1);

  \draw[thick, seq-red] (-0.760768, -0.0153293)
      ellipse[x radius=2.61837, y radius=1.84472, rotate=26.0068];
  \node[text=seq-red, font=\normalsize] at (0, -3.3)
    {$266 x^2 + 405 y^2 - 178 xy + 402 x - 123 y - 1374 = 0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
</image>
        <p>
          Now we consider the question of what quantity is minimized by this ellipse.  The least-squares solution <m>\hat x</m> minimizes the sum of the squares of the entries of the vector <m>b-A\hat x</m>, or equivalently, of <m>A\hat x-b</m>.  The vector <m>-b</m> contains the constant terms of the left-hand sides of <xref ref="leastsquares-ellipse-eq"/>, and
          <md>
<mrow>\def\eqline#1#2{\frac{405}{266}(#2)^2 - \frac{89}{133}(#1)(#2) + \frac{201}{133}(#1) - \frac{123}{266}(#2) - \frac{687}{133}}
\edef\eqs{\eqline02;
  \eqline21;
  \eqline1{-1};
  \eqline{-1}{-2};
  \eqline{-3}1;
  \eqline{-1}{-1}
}
\spalignsysdelims()
A\hat x =
\expandafter\syseq\expandafter{\eqs}</mrow>
</md>
          contains the rest of the terms on the left-hand side of <xref ref="leastsquares-ellipse-eq"/>.  Therefore, the entries of <m>A\hat x-b</m> are the quantities obtained by evaluating the function
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = x^2 + \frac{405}{266} y^2 -\frac{89}{133} xy + \frac{201}{133}x
            - \frac{123}{266}y - \frac{687}{133}</mrow>
</md>
          on the given data points.
        </p>
        <p>
          If our data points actually lay on the ellipse defined by <m>f(x,y)=0</m>, then evaluating <m>f(x,y)</m> on our data points would always yield zero, so <m>A\hat x-b</m> would be the zero vector.  This is not the case; instead, <m>A\hat x-b</m> contains the <em>actual</em> values of <m>f(x,y)</m> when evaluated on our data points.  The quantity being minimized is the sum of the squares of these values:
          <md>
<mrow>\amp\text{minimized} =</mrow>
<mrow>\amp\quad
            f(0,2)^2 + f(2,1)^2 + f(1,-1)^2 + f(-1,-2)^2 + f(-3,1)^2 + f(-1,-1)^2</mrow>
</md>.
          One way to visualize this is as follows.  We can put this best-fit problem into the framework of <xref ref="leastsquares-linear-eg"/> by asking to find an equation of the form
          <md>
<mrow>f(x,y) = x^2 + By^2 + Cxy + Dx + Ey + F</mrow>
</md>
          which best approximates the data table
          <md>
<mrow>\begin{array}{r|r|c}
  x \amp y \amp f(x,y) \\\hline
  0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
  2 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
  1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
  -1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
  -3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
  -1 \amp -1 \amp 0\rlap.
\end{array}</mrow>
</md>
          The resulting function minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances from these data points <m>(0,2,0),\,(2,1,0),\,\ldots</m>, which lie on the <m>xy</m>-plane, to the graph of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>The best-fit ellipse minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances (violet) from the points <m>(x,y,0)</m> to the graph of <m>f(x,y)</m> on the left.  The ellipse itself is the zero set of <m>f(x,y)</m>, on the right.  Click and drag the points on the right to see how the best-fit ellipse changes.  Can you arrange the points so that the best-fit conic section is actually a hyperbola?</caption>
          <interactive
              iframe="iframes/bestfit-implicit-if.html?func=x^2+A*y^2+B*x*y+C*x+D*y+EE&amp;v1=0,2&amp;v2=2,1&amp;v3=1,-1&amp;v4=-1,-2&amp;v5=-3,1&amp;v6=-1,1&amp;range=5&amp;rangez=25&amp;camera1=-2.14,.814,1.69"
              width="90%"
              aspect="4:3">
            <shortdescription>
              <!-- FILL: Brief alt-text for this interactive (linear algebra context) -->
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <!-- FILL: Complete accessibility description of this interactive for a linear algebra course -->
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </description>
            <instructions>
              <p>
                
              </p>
            </instructions>
          </interactive>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <note>
      <p>
        Gauss invented the method of least squares to find a best-fit ellipse:
he correctly predicted the (elliptical) orbit of the asteroid Ceres as it passed behind
the sun in 1801.
      </p>
    </note>

  </subsection>
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>True or false.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>For every set of three different points in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>, there is a unique best fit line.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>\hat{x}</m> is the least squares solution to <m>Ax = b</m> for an <m>m \times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, then <m>\hat{x}</m> is the closest point in <m>{\bf R}^n</m> to <m>b</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>If <m>\hat{x}</m> and <m>\hat{y}</m> are both least squares solutions to <m>Ax = b</m>, then <m>\hat{x} - \hat{y}</m> is in the null space of <m>A</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>In this problem, we follow the standard labeling of points in the
      <m>xy</m>-plane as <m>(x,y)</m>.</p>
      <p>Find the least-squares line
      <m>y = Mx + B</m> for the data points <m>(-4, -5)</m>, <m>(-1, 10)</m>, and <m>(2,
      7)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The (inconsistent) system of equations for the line through the
          three data points is:
        <md><mrow>-5 = -4M + B</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>10 = -1M + B</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>7 = 2M + B</mrow></md>.
        This is the matrix equation <m>Ax=b</m> where
          <m>A = \begin{pmatrix}-4 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 1\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>b= \begin{pmatrix}-5 \\ 10 \\ 7\end{pmatrix}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We need to solve <m>A^TA\hat{x} = A^T b</m>.
        <md><mrow>A^T A = \begin{pmatrix}21 \amp -3 \\ -3 \amp 3\end{pmatrix}, \qquad
            A^T b = \begin{pmatrix}24 \\ 12\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        We solve:
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}A^TA \amp \ \amp A^Tb\end{array}\right) = \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}21 \amp -3 \amp 24 \\ -3 \amp 3 \amp 12\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 18 \amp 108\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp -4 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 6\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 6\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>\hat{x} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 6\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Since we arranged the
          entries to correspond to <m>\begin{pmatrix}M \\ B\end{pmatrix}</m>, this means
        <md><mrow>\boxed{y = 2x + 6}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the best fit line through <m>(1, 0)</m>, <m>(2, 1)</m>, and <m>(3, 1)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find the best fit parabola through <m>(1, 0)</m>, <m>(2, 1)</m>, <m>(3, 1)</m>, and <m>(3, 0)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
</section>
